1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,720
This is ECE 358, video 19. The topic is distance-vector routing.

2
00:00:07,170 --> 00:00:23,277
After this video, you can use the Bellman-Ford equation to compute a distance vector. You can run the iterations of the slide example. You can explain what happens when a link cost changes. And you can explain count to infinity and its fixes.

3
00:00:23,727 --> 00:00:37,209
Slide 94 says that the lecture can skip distance vector. Check on Learn whether it is on your exam. The question bank has no distance vector question, so this video solves the slide examples.

4
00:00:37,659 --> 00:00:51,675
Distance-vector routing, also called the Routing Information Protocol, or RIP, uses the Bellman-Ford equation. D x of y is the cost of the least-cost path from node x to node y.

5
00:00:52,125 --> 00:00:59,592
x has neighbors v 1 to v k. c x v is the direct cost of the link from x to the neighbor v.

6
00:01:00,042 --> 00:01:07,530
D v of y is the estimated least cost from the neighbor v to y. Each neighbor has its own estimate.

7
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:19,628
Any path from x to y must leave x through one of its neighbors. So D x of y is the minimum, over all neighbors v, of c x v plus D v of y.

8
00:01:20,078 --> 00:01:25,006
The neighbor that gives the minimum is the next hop on the path from x to y.

9
00:01:25,456 --> 00:01:32,987
Slide 96 gives an example. We want D u of z. u has three neighbors: v, x and w.

10
00:01:33,437 --> 00:01:42,077
The neighbors know their costs to z. D v of z is 5, D x of z is 3, and D w of z is 3.

11
00:01:42,527 --> 00:01:48,521
First, the term through v. c u v plus D v of z is 2 plus 5, which is 7.

12
00:01:48,971 --> 00:01:54,113
Through x: c u x plus D x of z is 1 plus 3, which is 4.

13
00:01:54,563 --> 00:02:00,664
Through w: c u w plus D w of z is 5 plus 3, which is 8.

14
00:02:01,114 --> 00:02:05,829
The minimum of 7, 4 and 8 is 4. So D u of z is 4.

15
00:02:06,279 --> 00:02:11,911
x gives the minimum. So x is the next hop on the least-cost path from u to z.

16
00:02:12,361 --> 00:02:17,097
u does not need the full map. It needs only the vectors of its neighbors.

17
00:02:17,547 --> 00:02:27,637
Slide 97 gives the key idea. Each node keeps a distance vector. It is the list of its estimated costs to every node in the network.

18
00:02:28,087 --> 00:02:33,207
From time to time, each node sends its own distance vector to its neighbors.

19
00:02:33,657 --> 00:02:43,791
When node x receives a new distance vector from a neighbor, x updates its own vector. It uses the Bellman-Ford equation for each destination y.

20
00:02:44,241 --> 00:02:53,585
Under minor, natural conditions, the estimates converge to the actual least costs. No node ever needs the full map of the network.

21
00:02:54,035 --> 00:03:10,248
Slide 98 shows what each node does. It waits. It acts when a local link cost changes, or when a neighbor sends a new distance vector. So the algorithm is iterative and asynchronous. Nodes do not run in lock step.

22
00:03:10,698 --> 00:03:18,591
The node then recomputes its estimates with the Bellman-Ford equation. It uses the latest vectors from its neighbors.

23
00:03:19,041 --> 00:03:25,911
If its vector changed for any destination, the node notifies its neighbors. Then it waits again.

24
00:03:26,361 --> 00:03:36,857
So the algorithm is distributed. A node sends messages only when its vector changes. Its neighbors notify their own neighbors only if necessary.

25
00:03:37,307 --> 00:03:46,779
If no node receives a notification, every node stays in the wait state. So the algorithm stops by itself. It is self-stopping.

26
00:03:47,229 --> 00:03:54,546
Now the example of slides 99 to 111. Nine nodes, a to i, form a grid.

27
00:03:54,996 --> 00:04:00,692
At time t equals 0, each node knows only the costs to its direct neighbors.

28
00:04:01,142 --> 00:04:05,067
Look at the distance vector of node a. D a of a is 0.

29
00:04:05,517 --> 00:04:09,016
The neighbor b costs 8. So D a of b is 8.

30
00:04:09,466 --> 00:04:12,858
The neighbor d costs 1. So D a of d is 1.

31
00:04:13,308 --> 00:04:19,985
a has no direct link to c, e, f, g, h or i. So these entries are infinity.

32
00:04:20,435 --> 00:04:26,174
The example has two asymmetries. First, the link from a to b costs 8, not 1.

33
00:04:26,624 --> 00:04:29,397
Second, there is no link between c and f.

34
00:04:29,847 --> 00:04:36,055
Then all nodes send their distance vectors to their neighbors. The purple links carry these messages.

35
00:04:36,505 --> 00:04:45,295
Slides 100 to 105 show one iteration. First, all nodes receive the distance vectors of their neighbors.

36
00:04:45,745 --> 00:04:51,718
Second, all nodes compute their new local distance vector with the Bellman-Ford equation.

37
00:04:52,168 --> 00:04:58,077
Third, all nodes send their new vectors to their neighbors. Then the next iteration starts.

38
00:04:58,527 --> 00:05:08,106
The slides draw the iterations in lock step. At time t, each node uses the vectors that its neighbors had at time t minus 1.

39
00:05:08,556 --> 00:05:19,116
Now the computation inside the nodes. At t equals 1, node b receives the vectors of its neighbors a, c and e, from t equals 0.

40
00:05:19,566 --> 00:05:27,395
The table has one column for each neighbor v. Each term is the link cost c b v, plus D v of y.

41
00:05:27,845 --> 00:05:37,424
Destination a, the example on slide 108. Through a: 8 plus 0 is 8. Through c or e: 1 plus infinity.

42
00:05:37,874 --> 00:05:41,586
The minimum is 8, through a. So D b of a is 8.

43
00:05:42,036 --> 00:05:46,388
Destination b is b itself. D b of b is 0.

44
00:05:46,838 --> 00:05:53,579
Destination c. Through c: 1 plus 0 is 1. So D b of c is 1, through c.

45
00:05:54,029 --> 00:05:59,939
Destination d. Through a: 8 plus 1 is 9. Through e: 1 plus 1 is 2.

46
00:06:00,389 --> 00:06:04,527
The minimum is 2, through e. So b learned a new path to d.

47
00:06:04,977 --> 00:06:08,967
Destination e. Through e: 1 plus 0 is 1.

48
00:06:09,417 --> 00:06:14,878
Destination f. Through e: 1 plus 1 is 2. f is a neighbor of e.

49
00:06:15,328 --> 00:06:22,581
Destination g. All three terms are infinity. No neighbor of b knew g at t equals 0.

50
00:06:23,031 --> 00:06:26,551
Destination h. Through e: 1 plus 1 is 2.

51
00:06:27,001 --> 00:06:32,313
Destination i is also infinity. g and i need more iterations.

52
00:06:32,763 --> 00:06:37,499
The new vector of b at t equals 1 is the column D b of y.

53
00:06:37,949 --> 00:06:46,909
At t equals 1, node c receives one vector, from b. c has only one neighbor, because there is no link from c to f.

54
00:06:47,359 --> 00:06:51,839
So each entry is c c b, which is 1, plus D b of y.

55
00:06:52,289 --> 00:06:55,937
Destination a: 1 plus 8 is 9, through b.

56
00:06:56,387 --> 00:06:59,566
Destination b: 1 plus 0 is 1.

57
00:07:00,016 --> 00:07:04,347
Destination c is c itself. D c of c is 0.

58
00:07:04,797 --> 00:07:11,773
Destination d: 1 plus infinity is infinity. b did not know d at t equals 0.

59
00:07:12,223 --> 00:07:15,700
Destination e: 1 plus 1 is 2, through b.

60
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:21,718
For f, g, h and i, b had infinity. So these entries stay at infinity.

61
00:07:22,168 --> 00:07:29,805
Slide 110 has one error. Its box labelled D V in c swaps the values for d and e.

62
00:07:30,255 --> 00:07:37,551
The computed lines on the same slide are right. D c of d is infinity, and D c of e is 2.

63
00:07:38,001 --> 00:07:45,404
Slide 111 asks a question. What is the new distance vector of node e at t equals 1?

64
00:07:45,854 --> 00:07:51,678
e receives the t equals 0 vectors of its neighbors b, d, f and h.

65
00:07:52,128 --> 00:07:53,771
Pause the video and try it.

66
00:07:54,221 --> 00:08:02,455
Destination a. Through b: 1 plus 8 is 9. Through d: 1 plus 1 is 2. Through f and h: infinity.

67
00:08:02,905 --> 00:08:06,361
The minimum is 2. So D e of a is 2, through d.

68
00:08:06,811 --> 00:08:10,843
Destination b. Through b: 1 plus 0 is 1.

69
00:08:11,293 --> 00:08:14,899
Destination c. Through b: 1 plus 1 is 2.

70
00:08:15,349 --> 00:08:19,381
Destination d. Through d: 1 plus 0 is 1.

71
00:08:19,831 --> 00:08:24,076
Destination e is e itself. D e of e is 0.

72
00:08:24,526 --> 00:08:28,686
Destination f. Through f: 1 plus 0 is 1.

73
00:08:29,136 --> 00:08:35,941
Destination g. Through d: 1 plus 1 is 2. Through h: 1 plus 1 is also 2.

74
00:08:36,391 --> 00:08:42,237
Two neighbors give the same minimum. Either one can be the next hop. The table writes d.

75
00:08:42,687 --> 00:08:46,633
Destination h. Through h: 1 plus 0 is 1.

76
00:08:47,083 --> 00:08:51,350
Destination i. Through f or h: 1 plus 1 is 2.

77
00:08:51,800 --> 00:08:58,221
The answer is the column D e of y: 2, 1, 2, 1, 0, 1, 2, 1 and 2.

78
00:08:58,671 --> 00:09:05,583
e is in the center of the grid. After one iteration, it already knows a finite cost to every node.

79
00:09:06,033 --> 00:09:10,599
Practice. Find the new distance vector of node a at t equals 1.

80
00:09:11,049 --> 00:09:15,635
a receives the t equals 0 vectors of its neighbors b and d.

81
00:09:16,085 --> 00:09:17,728
Pause the video and try it.

82
00:09:18,178 --> 00:09:21,762
Destination a is a itself. D a of a is 0.

83
00:09:22,212 --> 00:09:28,676
Destination b. Through b: 8 plus 0 is 8. Through d: 1 plus infinity.

84
00:09:29,126 --> 00:09:37,809
So D a of b stays at 8. d did not know b at t equals 0. So the cheaper path through d is not found yet.

85
00:09:38,259 --> 00:09:42,141
Destination c. Through b: 8 plus 1 is 9.

86
00:09:42,591 --> 00:09:46,623
Destination d. Through d: 1 plus 0 is 1.

87
00:09:47,073 --> 00:09:50,657
Destination e. Through d: 1 plus 1 is 2.

88
00:09:51,107 --> 00:09:54,371
Destination f. Both terms are infinity.

89
00:09:54,821 --> 00:09:58,555
Destination g. Through d: 1 plus 1 is 2.

90
00:09:59,005 --> 00:10:02,738
Destinations h and i. Both terms are infinity.

91
00:10:03,188 --> 00:10:05,833
The answer is the column D a of y.

92
00:10:06,283 --> 00:10:15,350
The slides stop after t equals 2 and say: and so on. Here are the next iterations for node a, computed with the same rule.

93
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:20,024
At t equals 0, a knows only its neighbors b and d.

94
00:10:20,474 --> 00:10:25,274
At t equals 1, a learns c at 9: 8 plus 1, through b.

95
00:10:25,724 --> 00:10:29,991
a also learns e and g at 2: 1 plus 1, through d.

96
00:10:30,441 --> 00:10:34,494
At t equals 2, D a of b drops from 8 to 3.

97
00:10:34,944 --> 00:10:44,800
The new path is a, d, e, b. Its cost is 1 plus 1 plus 1, which is 3. The expensive direct link is no longer used.

98
00:10:45,250 --> 00:10:47,959
a also learns f and h at cost 3.

99
00:10:48,409 --> 00:10:54,937
At t equals 3, D a of c drops from 9 to 4, along the path a, d, e, b, c.

100
00:10:55,387 --> 00:10:57,777
a also learns i at cost 4.

101
00:10:58,227 --> 00:11:01,256
Now a knows a finite cost to every node.

102
00:11:01,706 --> 00:11:09,941
At t equals 4, no vector changes. The row for t equals 4 is the same as the row for t equals 3.

103
00:11:10,391 --> 00:11:14,209
So no node sends a message, and the algorithm stops.

104
00:11:14,659 --> 00:11:21,763
These values are computed with the lock-step model that the slides use. They are the true least costs of the graph.

105
00:11:22,213 --> 00:11:30,533
Slide 112 shows how information spreads. At t equals 0, the state of c is known only at c.

106
00:11:30,983 --> 00:11:37,490
At t equals 1, it has reached b. It can change distance vectors up to 1 hop away.

107
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:43,487
At t equals 2, it can also change the vectors at a and e, 2 hops away.

108
00:11:43,937 --> 00:11:48,758
At t equals 3, it reaches d, f and h, 3 hops away.

109
00:11:49,208 --> 00:11:55,864
f is close to c, but there is no link c f. So the information goes around, through b and e.

110
00:11:56,314 --> 00:12:00,879
At t equals 4, it reaches g and i, 4 hops away.

111
00:12:01,329 --> 00:12:07,985
So the label of each node is its hop count from c. Information moves one hop per iteration.

112
00:12:08,435 --> 00:12:14,942
The slide writes c, f, h for t equals 3. c is the source, so the slide means d.

113
00:12:15,392 --> 00:12:24,928
Now link cost changes, slide 113. Three nodes: x, y and z. Before the change, the link x y costs 4.

114
00:12:25,378 --> 00:12:28,770
So D y of x is 4, on the direct link.

115
00:12:29,220 --> 00:12:37,113
D z of x is 5, through y. 1 plus 4 is 5, and 5 is less than the direct cost of 50.

116
00:12:37,563 --> 00:12:42,470
At time t 0, the cost of link x y drops from 4 to 1.

117
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:51,005
y detects the change. y computes the minimum of 1, and 1 plus 5. So D y of x becomes 1.

118
00:12:51,455 --> 00:12:53,973
y sends its new vector to its neighbors.

119
00:12:54,423 --> 00:12:57,729
At t 1, z receives the update from y.

120
00:12:58,179 --> 00:13:05,966
z computes 1 plus 1, which is 2. 2 is less than 50. So D z of x becomes 2, through y.

121
00:13:06,416 --> 00:13:08,955
z sends its new vector to its neighbors.

122
00:13:09,405 --> 00:13:18,919
At t 2, y receives the vector of z. 1 plus 2 is 3, which is not less than 1. So D y of x stays at 1.

123
00:13:19,369 --> 00:13:25,833
y's least costs did not change. So y does not send a message, and the algorithm stops.

124
00:13:26,283 --> 00:13:34,859
The good news spread with two updates: y at t 0, and z at t 1. The slide says: good news travels fast.

125
00:13:35,309 --> 00:13:42,925
Slide 114 shows the opposite case. The cost of link x y rises from 4 to 60.

126
00:13:43,375 --> 00:13:49,583
Before the change, D y of x is 4, direct. D z of x is 5, through y.

127
00:13:50,033 --> 00:13:53,532
y sees that its direct link to x now costs 60.

128
00:13:53,982 --> 00:13:57,545
But z has said that it has a path to x at cost 5.

129
00:13:57,995 --> 00:14:05,355
So y computes the minimum of 60, and 1 plus 5. That is 6, through z. This is update 1.

130
00:14:05,805 --> 00:14:09,751
y does not know that the path of z goes through y itself.

131
00:14:10,201 --> 00:14:13,039
y notifies z of its new cost, 6.

132
00:14:13,489 --> 00:14:21,169
z learns that its path through y now costs 6. z computes 1 plus 6, which is 7, through y.

133
00:14:21,619 --> 00:14:28,275
y learns the new cost 7 from z. y computes 1 plus 7, which is 8, through z.

134
00:14:28,725 --> 00:14:32,415
z computes 1 plus 8, which is 9, through y.

135
00:14:32,865 --> 00:14:41,100
Each update adds only 1. So each node's value grows by only 2 per exchange: from 6 to 8 for y.

136
00:14:41,550 --> 00:14:47,950
During this time, packets for x go back and forth between y and z. This is a routing loop.

137
00:14:48,400 --> 00:14:57,445
The values climb. In update 45, y computes the minimum of 60, and 1 plus 49. That is 50, through z.

138
00:14:57,895 --> 00:15:05,277
Now z compares its direct link, 50, with 1 plus 50 through y. The minimum is 50, direct.

139
00:15:05,727 --> 00:15:11,956
Finally, y computes 1 plus 50, which is 51, through z. The routes are right again.

140
00:15:12,406 --> 00:15:19,403
Count the updates. y takes the values 6, 8, and so on up to 50. That is 23 updates.

141
00:15:19,853 --> 00:15:26,232
z takes the values 7, 9, and so on up to 49. That is 22 updates.

142
00:15:26,682 --> 00:15:36,047
Then z changes to 50, and y changes to 51. That is 2 more. 23 plus 22 plus 2 is 47.

143
00:15:36,497 --> 00:15:47,335
So, counted one update at a time, the routes are right only after 47 updates. This problem is called count to infinity. Bad news travels slow.

144
00:15:47,785 --> 00:15:55,529
Slide 115 shows the same problem with three nodes in a line: X, A and B. Each link costs 1.

145
00:15:55,979 --> 00:15:59,883
Before the failure, A reaches X directly, with cost 1.

146
00:16:00,333 --> 00:16:04,599
B reaches X through A, with cost 1 plus 1, which is 2.

147
00:16:05,049 --> 00:16:09,892
Now the link from X to A fails. A sets its cost to X to infinity.

148
00:16:10,342 --> 00:16:13,521
But B has said that it reaches X at cost 2.

149
00:16:13,971 --> 00:16:22,269
A does not know that the path of B goes through A. So A computes 1 plus 2, which is 3, and routes to X through B.

150
00:16:22,719 --> 00:16:26,239
B receives the update from A: X at cost 3.

151
00:16:26,689 --> 00:16:33,303
The next hop of B to X is A. So B must use the new cost of A. 1 plus 3 is 4.

152
00:16:33,753 --> 00:16:37,209
Then A computes 5, B computes 6, and so on.

153
00:16:37,659 --> 00:16:45,851
The costs grow until they reach the value that means infinity. Only then do both nodes know that X is not reachable.

154
00:16:46,301 --> 00:16:49,031
This is count to infinity between two nodes.

155
00:16:49,481 --> 00:16:54,623
Slide 116 gives two fixes. The first is split horizon.

156
00:16:55,073 --> 00:16:59,958
B learned its path to X from A. So B does not tell A about this path.

157
00:17:00,408 --> 00:17:05,763
A never hears the old cost 2. So A does not pick B, and A keeps infinity.

158
00:17:06,213 --> 00:17:15,493
The second fix is poisoned reverse. If a node routes to X through a neighbor, it tells that neighbor that its own cost to X is infinity.

159
00:17:15,943 --> 00:17:20,551
B routes to X through A. So B tells A: X at infinity.

160
00:17:21,001 --> 00:17:26,697
1 plus infinity is infinity. So A does not try to route to X back through B.

161
00:17:27,147 --> 00:17:34,997
With either fix, A keeps infinity and tells B. B then sets infinity too. The two-node loop does not form.

162
00:17:35,447 --> 00:17:46,135
Apply poisoned reverse to the network of slide 114. z routes to x through y. So z tells y that its cost to x is infinity.

163
00:17:46,585 --> 00:17:54,585
When the link cost becomes 60, y computes the minimum of 60, and 1 plus infinity. That is 60, direct.

164
00:17:55,035 --> 00:18:02,459
y sends 60 to z. z computes the minimum of 50, and 1 plus 60. That is 50, direct.

165
00:18:02,909 --> 00:18:10,803
z sends 50 to y. y computes the minimum of 60, and 1 plus 50. That is 51, through z.

166
00:18:11,253 --> 00:18:15,583
So the routes are right after 3 updates, instead of 47.

167
00:18:16,033 --> 00:18:24,993
Unfortunately, these fixes do not solve the loop problem in general. Loops of three or four nodes can still count to infinity.

168
00:18:25,443 --> 00:18:32,185
Here is the method for a distance vector question. First, list the neighbors of x and their link costs.

169
00:18:32,635 --> 00:18:37,563
Second, use the vectors of the neighbors from the previous time, t minus 1.

170
00:18:38,013 --> 00:18:49,341
Third, for each destination, write one term per neighbor: the link cost plus the estimate of the neighbor. Take the minimum, and record the neighbor that gives it as the next hop.

171
00:18:49,791 --> 00:18:54,527
D x of x is 0, and infinity plus anything is infinity.

172
00:18:54,977 --> 00:19:01,889
Common errors. First, using a node that is not a neighbor. In the example, c has no link to f.

173
00:19:02,339 --> 00:19:06,819
Second, using the new values of the neighbors from the same time step.

174
00:19:07,269 --> 00:19:21,413
Third, keeping the old estimate in the minimum. The Bellman-Ford equation uses only the terms of the neighbors. When a link cost rises, the old estimate is wrong, as slide 114 shows.

175
00:19:21,863 --> 00:19:26,193
Fourth, forgetting that the next hop is the neighbor that gives the minimum.

176
00:19:26,643 --> 00:19:33,790
To check your answer, the converged vector must equal the least costs from Dijkstra's algorithm on the same graph.

177
00:19:34,240 --> 00:19:50,453
Here is the recap. The Bellman-Ford equation gives D x of y as the minimum over the neighbors of the link cost plus the neighbor's estimate. The neighbor that gives the minimum is the next hop. Each node knows only the vectors of its neighbors.

178
00:19:50,903 --> 00:19:53,762
Information moves one hop per iteration.

179
00:19:54,212 --> 00:20:14,713
When a cost drops, the news spreads fast. When a cost rises, the estimates can count to infinity and form routing loops. Split horizon and poisoned reverse fix loops of two nodes, but not all loops. The next video covers hierarchical routing, OSPF areas and BGP.
